{"name":"Dingy Dungeon","hex":"#c53151","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dingy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Dark, dull.","Shabby, squalid, uncared-for."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of dinghy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dingy"},{"term":"dungeon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An underground prison or vault, typically built underneath a castle.","The low area between two drumlins."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To imprison in a dungeon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dungeon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dungeon","blocks":[{"text":"A dungeon is a type of prison, usually an underground room or cell, in which prisoners are held. Dungeons are generally associated with medieval castles. They are closely connected in concept both to the prison and the oubliette, and the words are sometimes used interchangeably. In the popular imagination, a dungeon is claustrophobic, strong, damp, and located in a castle under the direct control of a medieval lord. This intentional unpleasantness and personal control over a dungeon contrasts to the modern prison, which provides a civil function."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/BlarneyCastle5.jpg/330px-BlarneyCastle5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/BlarneyCastle5.jpg/500px-BlarneyCastle5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/BlarneyCastle5.jpg/1280px-BlarneyCastle5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/BlarneyCastle5.jpg/500px-BlarneyCastle5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt"